<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barcelona May Days</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as May Days of 1937 · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> May Days were street fighting in Barcelona from 3 to 8 May 1937 between anarchists and the POUM on one side and Republican and Communist forces on the other. They began when police tried to take over the CNT-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> exchange, and several hundred people were killed. The outcome weakened the anarchists, brought down Largo Caballero's government and led to the suppression of the POUM.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barcelona-may-days.jpg" alt="Barcelona May Days" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Spanish Revolution · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:POUM_FAI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the military rising of July 1936 failed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, the city and the rest of Catalonia came under the control of workers' militias, chiefly those of the anarchist union CNT-FAI and the socialist UGT, and industries were collectivised. The Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its Catalan counterpart, the Unified Socialist Party of Catalonia (PSUC), held that the war had to be won before any revolution, while the POUM and the anarchists wanted to carry out the revolution during the war. Through the spring of 1937 tension rose: on 26 March Josep Tarradellas ordered political organisations to hand over their weapons, and on 25 April Carabineros took the customs house at Puigcerdà from CNT patrols in a clash that killed its mayor, Antonio Martin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 3 May about 200 police under Eusebio Rodríguez Salas, Catalan minister of public order, tried to take the Telefónica exchange, which an anarcho-syndicalist committee held under a decree of the Generalitat itself. Shooting broke out, and within hours several hundred barricades rose across the city: the PSUC and the government held the districts east of the Ramblas, the anarchists the west and the suburbs.</p><p>On 4 May the anarchist ministers Joan García Oliver and Federica Montseny appealed on the radio for their followers to lay down their arms. On 5 May the anarchist writers Camillo Berneri and Francesco Barbieri were arrested and murdered, and fighting spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortosa">Tortosa</a> and other towns. A force of about 5,000, most of them Assault Guards, arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> on 7 May and disarmed and arrested many members of the CNT, FAI, Libertarian Youth and POUM. By 8 May the streets were calm. The officially given toll was 400 dead and 1,000 wounded; detailed later studies counted 218 dead in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The days showed that the anarchists no longer spoke with one voice: their ministers, intent on winning the war, had lost touch with their youth, who put the revolution first. The Republican government outlawed the POUM on 16 June and arrested its leaders, among them Julián Gorkin and Andreu Nin. Largo Caballero's government fell and the four anarchist ministers left it, a clear gain in power for the Communists. George Orwell, who served with the POUM, described the fighting in Homage to Catalonia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “May Days” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>May Days of 1937</b> weakened the anarchists, the Negrín government dissolved the council in August 1937.</blockquote><a href="/state/regional-defence-council-of-aragon">Regional Defence Council of Aragon</a></li></ul></section>
